RUN: %COMPILE_IR %S/function_pins.cfc %S/myAdd.st | %CHECK_IR %s

The diagram, with execution orders in parentheses; `o` marks a negated pin:

           +-------- myAdd ---------+
a1 ------o-| IN1              myAdd |-o------> b1 (2)
a2 --------| IN2       myAddDoubled |-o------> b2 (1)
           +------------------------+ (0)

Each ordered element must get a debug location in the diagram's own file,
at line = order + 1, so a debugger can break on it.

Debug file per area: declaration text vs. diagram `function_pins`.
CHECK-DAG: ![[#DECL:]] = !DIFile(filename: "function_pins.cfc", directory:
CHECK-DAG: ![[#DIAGRAM:]] = !DIFile(filename: "function_pins.cfc.function_pins", directory:

The function lives in the declaration; a lexical block inside it is bound to the diagram file.
CHECK-DAG: ![[#SP:]] = distinct !DISubprogram(name: "function_pins", {{.*}}, file: ![[#DECL]], line: 1,
CHECK-DAG: ![[#LB:]] = distinct !DILexicalBlock(scope: ![[#SP]], file: ![[#DIAGRAM]])

Diagram statements are scoped to that block, line = execution order + 1 (0 is reserved).
CHECK-DAG: ![[#CALL:]] = !DILocation(line: 1, scope: ![[#LB]])
CHECK-DAG: ![[#B2:]] = !DILocation(line: 2, scope: ![[#LB]])
CHECK-DAG: ![[#B1:]] = !DILocation(line: 3, scope: ![[#LB]])

Block `myAdd` (order 0), sink `b2` (order 1), sink `b1` (order 2).
CHECK-DAG: define void @function_pins(ptr %0) !dbg ![[#SP]]
CHECK-DAG: call i32 @myAdd({{.*}}), !dbg ![[#CALL]]
CHECK-DAG: store i32 {{.*}}, ptr %b2, {{.*}}!dbg ![[#B2]]
CHECK-DAG: store i32 {{.*}}, ptr %b1, {{.*}}!dbg ![[#B1]]

Declared variables keep their text line; the captured block output sits at its block's order.
CHECK-DAG: !DIDerivedType(tag: DW_TAG_member, name: "a1", scope: ![[#DECL]], file: ![[#DECL]], line: 3,
CHECK-DAG: !DIDerivedType(tag: DW_TAG_member, name: "__out_myAdd_1", scope: ![[#DECL]], file: ![[#DIAGRAM]], line: 1,
